","The relationship used to estimate the flilacity limits of mixtures from the data for individual fluids is known as the **Hückley-Hückbach-Hückbach (HHH) equation**, also known as the **Hückbach equation**.  

The Hückbach equation is a semi-ideal equation that is used to estimate the fluracity limits of a mixture by averaging the flflenenes of the individual components.  

For **H₂**:  
\[
\text{F}_{\text{mix}} = \frac{\sum \text{F}_{\text{mix}} \times \text{mole fraction of the component}}{\sum \text{mole fraction of the component}}
\]  
where \( F_{\text{mix}} \) is the flurability level of the mixture, and \( F_{\text{mix}} \) is the florability level of the component.  

For **NCO**:  
\[
\text{F}_{\text{mix}} = \sum \text{F}_{\text{mix}} \times \text{mole fraction of the component}
\]  

**Note**: The Hösbach equation is valid for mixtures of gases with similar viscosities and viscosibilities.  

The Hückbach equation is a simplified model and is not accurate for all mixtures.  

\boxed{\text{Hückbach equation}}",ground_truth_similarity,"# Task Description
