- \( g = 9 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) (acceleration due to gravity),
- \( m = 3600 / 9 = 400 \, \text{kg} \) (mass of the satellite).

The kinetic energy \( K \) of the satellite is given by:

\[
K = \frac{1}{2} m v^2
\]

where:
- \( m = 400 \, \text{kg} \) (mass of the satellite),
- \( v = 800 \, \text{m/s} \) (relative velocity).

