- \(\tau = 2\pi m r^2 \omega^2\) is the torque,
- \(I = \frac{1}{2}mr^2\) is the moment of inertia of the disc-like pulley,
- \alpha = \alpha = \frac{2a}{r}\) is the angular acceleration,
- \(a = 2 \, \text{m/s}^2\) is the tangential acceleration of point P,
- \(r = 0.5 \, \text{m}\) is the radius of the pulley,
- \(m = 1 \, \text{kg}\) is the mass of the pulley.

First, calculate the moment of inertia \(I\):
\[
I = \frac{1}{2}mr^2 = \frac{1}{2}(1 \, \text{kg})(0.5 \, \text{m})^2 = 0.025 \, \text{kg·m}^2
\]

Next, calculate the angular acceleration \(\alpha\):
\[
\alpha = \frac{a}{r} = \frac{2 \, \text{m/s}^2}{0.5 \, \text{m}} = 4 \, \text{rad/s}^2
\]

Now, calculate the torque \(\tau\):
\[
\tau = I \alpha = (0.025 \, \text{kg·m}^2)(4 \, \text{rad/s}^2) = 0.1 \, \text{N·m}
\]

The torque due to the force \(F\) is:
\[
\tau = F r
\]

Rearranging to solve for \(F\):
\[
F = \frac{\tau}{r} = \frac{0.1 \, \text{N·m}}{0.5 \, \text{m}} = 0.2 \, \text{N}
\]

Thus, the magnitude of the force \(F\) is \(\boxed{0.2 \, \text{N}}\).
